aquinas-ontological-objection.md:1: argue/readings: S7 is read 2 ways: aquinas-ontological-objection.logic.r2 and aquinas-ontological-objection.logic.r2.2 have "exists_mentally"; aquinas-ontological-objection.logic.r2.3 has "exists_mentally" and "meaning ∧ actual_greater ∧ exists_mentally → known_once_terms"; they differ when only "by the word "God" is signified that thing than which nothing greater can be conceived", "that which exists actually and mentally is greater than that which exists only mentally" and "as soon as the word "God" is understood, it exists mentally" are true, which the first allows
aquinas-ontological-objection.md:1: argue/answered: S7: answered yes, which keeps aquinas-ontological-objection.logic.r2 and aquinas-ontological-objection.logic.r2.2, to: Suppose "by the word "God" is signified that thing than which nothing greater can be conceived", "that which exists actually and mentally is greater than that which exists only mentally" and "as soon as the word "God" is understood, it exists mentally" are true and "as soon as the signification of the word "God" is understood, it is at once seen that God exists" is false. Is S7, "Therefore, since as soon as the word "God" is understood it exists mentally, it also follows that it exists actually.", true then?
